Theorem 9.8.6 . 373CA5388E72 Let $X$ be a compact complex space. Every prime divisor over $X$ is represented on a smooth model obtained from $X$ by a finite sequence of blowups with smooth centers. Its representative may also be chosen smooth. Proof. After reducing to the case of a manifold, we blow up the image of the divisor in $X$ as long as it has codimension at least two; each step lowers the order of vanishing along the divisor of the Jacobian determinant of the model. Step 1: reduction to a manifold. Let $\widehat X	o X$ be a resolution given by theorem 9.6.6 , a finite sequence of blowups with smooth centers, since $X$ is compact. Let the prime divisor be represented by $(M_0,F_0)$, and let $M$ be a common smooth model of $M_0$ and $\widehat X$, with $E\subset M$ the strict transform of $F_0$ (proposition 9.8.3 ); then $(M,E)$ represents the same divisor, as we see on the common smooth model $M$. A finite sequence of blowups with smooth centers over $\widehat X$, composed with $\widehat X	o X$, is such a sequence over $X$, and smooth models of $\widehat X$ are smooth models of $X$, by corollary 9.7.3 . Hence we may assume that $X$ is a compact complex manifold, and we are given a proper bimeromorphic map $f\colon M	o X$ from a compact manifold and an irreducible hypersurface $E\subset M$. Since $E$ is irreducible, hence connected, it lies in a connected component $M_ E$ of $M$, and $f(M_ E)$ lies in a connected component $X_ E$ of $X$. These components have the same dimension $n$, because $M_ E$ contains a nonempty open set mapped isomorphically onto an open subset of $X$ (lemma 9.4.1 ); moreover $n\geq 1$ and $\dim E=n-1$, since $E$ is a nonempty hypersurface. If $n=1$, the center $f(E)$ considered below is a point of $X_ E$, hence a hypersurface. Step 2: the order of the Jacobian. The Jacobian determinant $J_ f$ of $f$ in local coordinates is holomorphic and determined up to multiplication by holomorphic units. It does not vanish identically near any point, because $f$ is an isomorphism over a dense open subset of $X$ whose inverse image is dense in $M$ (lemma 9.4.1 ). Its order of vanishing $\operatorname {ord}_ EJ_ f$ along $E$ is the largest $k$ such that $J_ f$ is divisible by the $k$th power of a local equation of $E$ near points of $E_{\mathrm{reg}}$; definition 6.12.3 uses the same notion of order along a hypersurface. It is a finite nonnegative integer $k$, since a function divisible by all powers of a local coordinate $y$ has vanishing Taylor coefficients in $y$ and vanishes near the point. It does not depend on the point of $E_{\mathrm{reg}}$, which is a connected manifold by corollary 9.3.17 : for each integer $j$, the set of points of $E_{\mathrm{reg}}$ at which the order is at least $j$ is open, and it is closed by the identity theorem on $E_{\mathrm{reg}}$, since a holomorphic function vanishing on $E$ near a regular point is divisible by a local equation of $E$. For composable maps the chain rule gives D52CB808B809 9.8.7  egin{equation}  \operatorname {ord}_ E J_{g\circ h} =\operatorname {ord}_ E J_ h+ \operatorname {ord}_ E(J_ g\circ h), \end{equation} because orders of vanishing along $E$ add under multiplication. Replacing $M$ by a common smooth model $h\colon M'	o M$ and $E$ by its strict transform $E'$ does not change $k$: by proposition 9.8.3 , $h$ is an isomorphism near generic points of $E'$, so $\operatorname {ord}_{E'}J_ h=0$ and $\operatorname {ord}_{E'}(J_ f\circ h)=k$. Step 3: one principalization lowers the order. The center $Z=f(E)$ is an analytic subset of $X$ by theorem 9.7.2 , of dimension at most $\dim E=n-1$ by lemma 9.3.15 (3). It is irreducible: if $Z=Z_1\cup Z_2$ with analytic subsets $Z_1,Z_2$, then the irreducible set $E$ is the union of its analytic subsets $E\cap f^{-1}(Z_1)$ and $E\cap f^{-1}(Z_2)$, so it lies in one of them, and $Z$ equals $Z_1$ or $Z_2$. The same argument shows that images of irreducible analytic sets under proper holomorphic maps are irreducible. Suppose first that $Z$ is a hypersurface, and let $S\subset X$ be as in proposition 9.8.3 for $f$. The strict transform $Z^ M$ of $Z$ in $M$ is an irreducible hypersurface containing $f^{-1}(Z\setminus S)$. The set $E\cap f^{-1}(Z\setminus S)$ is open in $E$, and it is nonempty, because $f(E)=Z$ is not contained in $S$, which has codimension at least two. So $E\cap Z^ M$ is an analytic subset of $E$ with interior points in $E$, and $E\subset Z^ M$ by corollary 9.3.17 (2); then $E=Z^ M$ by corollary 9.3.17 (3). Hence $(X,Z)$ represents the given divisor, as we see on the common smooth model $M$. Suppose now that $Z$ has codimension at least two. Its ideal $\mathcal I_ Z$ is coherent by theorem 9.3.10 , and it is not identically zero on any component of $X$, since $Z$ is nowhere dense. Let $\sigma =\sigma _1\circ \cdots \circ \sigma _ t\colon X'	o X$ be the finite sequence of blowups with centers $C_ i\subset X_{i-1}$ and transformed ideals $\mathfrak a_ i$, $\mathfrak a_0=\mathcal I_ Z$, given by theorem 9.6.7 ; it is proper and bimeromorphic by corollary 9.7.3 , so $X'$ is a smooth model of $X$. Let $h\colon M'	o M$ and $f'\colon M'	o X'$ be the maps from a common smooth model of $M$ and $X'$, so that $f\circ h=\sigma \circ f'$, let $E'$ be the strict transform of $E$, let $ho _ i\colon M'	o X_ i$ be the induced maps, and let $Z_ i=ho _ i(E')$; thus $Z_0=Z$. Let $r\geq 2$ be the codimension of $Z$, which is pure by corollary 9.3.17 (1). We claim that for some $i$ the center $C_ i$ contains $Z_{i-1}$, and that the connected component of $C_ i$ containing $Z_{i-1}$ has codimension $r$. The sets $Z_ j$ are irreducible, as images of the irreducible set $E'$, and $\sigma _{j+1}(Z_{j+1})=Z_ j$. Put $	au _ j=\sigma _1\circ \cdots \circ \sigma _ j$. Suppose that $Z_{l-1}
ot\subset C_ l$ for all $l\leq j$. We show by induction on $j$ that there are a nowhere dense analytic subset $A_ j\subset Z$ and an open set $N_ j\subset X\setminus A_ j$ containing $Z^\circ _ j=Z\setminus A_ j$ such that $	au _ j$ is an isomorphism over $N_ j$ and, on $	au _ j^{-1}(N_ j)$, \[  Z_ j=	au _ j^{-1}(Z^\circ _ j),\qquad \mathfrak a_ j=\mathcal I_ Z\mathcal O_{X_ j}.  \] For $j=0$ take $A_0=\varnothing $ and $N_0=X$. Assume the assertion for some $j<t$, and suppose that $Z_ j
ot\subset C_{j+1}$. On $	au _ j^{-1}(N_ j)$ we have $C_{j+1}\subset V(\mathfrak a_ j)=	au _ j^{-1}(Z^\circ _ j)\subset Z_ j$. By corollary 9.3.17 (2), $C_{j+1}\cap Z_ j$ is nowhere dense in $Z_ j$. Its image $	au _ j(C_{j+1}\cap Z_ j)\subset Z$ is analytic by theorem 9.7.2 , and it is nowhere dense in $Z$: its part in $Z^\circ _ j$ corresponds under the isomorphism $	au _ j$ to the nowhere dense part of $C_{j+1}\cap Z_ j$ in the open subset $	au _ j^{-1}(Z^\circ _ j)$ of $Z_ j$, and $A_ j$ is nowhere dense. Let $A_{j+1}=A_ j\cup 	au _ j(C_{j+1}\cap Z_ j)$ and $N_{j+1}=N_ j\setminus A_{j+1}$. Then $	au _ j^{-1}(N_{j+1})$ does not meet $C_{j+1}$, so $\sigma _{j+1}$ is an isomorphism over it and does not change the ideal there: $\mathfrak a_{j+1}=\mathcal O_{X_{j+1}}(E_{j+1})\mathfrak a_ j\mathcal O_{X_{j+1}}=\mathcal I_ Z\mathcal O_{X_{j+1}}$ on $	au _{j+1}^{-1}(N_{j+1})$. Moreover $Z_{j+1}\cap 	au _{j+1}^{-1}(N_{j+1})=\sigma _{j+1}^{-1}(Z_ j\cap 	au _ j^{-1}(N_{j+1}))=	au _{j+1}^{-1}(Z^\circ _{j+1})$, because $\sigma _{j+1}(Z_{j+1})=Z_ j$ and $\sigma _{j+1}$ is injective there. This completes the induction. Since $A_ j$ is nowhere dense in $Z$, the set $Z^\circ _ j$ is nonempty, and so is $	au _ j^{-1}(Z^\circ _ j)$. If $Z_{l-1}
ot\subset C_ l$ held for all $l\leq t$, the induction would reach $j=t$, and $\mathfrak a_ t$ would be the ideal of the nonempty set $	au _ t^{-1}(Z^\circ _ t)$ on $	au _ t^{-1}(N_ t)$, whereas $\mathfrak a_ t$ is the unit ideal. So there is a least index $i$ with $Z_{i-1}\subset C_ i$, and the assertion holds for $j=i-1$. On $	au _{i-1}^{-1}(N_{i-1})$ we have $Z_{i-1}\subset C_ i\subset V(\mathfrak a_{i-1})=	au _{i-1}^{-1}(Z^\circ _{i-1})\subset Z_{i-1}$, so $C_ i$ coincides there with a copy of the open subset $Z^\circ _{i-1}$ of $Z$, which is pure of codimension $r$. The set $Z_{i-1}$ is connected, being the closure of the connected manifold $(Z_{i-1})_{\mathrm{reg}}$, so it lies in one connected component of the manifold $C_ i$, and this component has codimension $r$. By ( 9.6.4 ) and proposition 9.6.3 , near points over this component the Jacobian determinant of $\sigma _ i$ is, in any local coordinates, a unit times the $(r-1)$st power of a local equation of the exceptional divisor $E_ i=\sigma _ i^{-1}(C_ i)$. Since $Z_ i=ho _ i(E')$ lies over $Z_{i-1}\subset C_ i$, the function $J_{\sigma _ i}\circ ho _ i$ vanishes on $E'$; it does not vanish identically near any point, because $J_{\sigma _ i}$ vanishes only on $E_ i$, and $ho _ i^{-1}(E_ i)$ is nowhere dense, $ho _ i$ being proper and bimeromorphic as the composite of $f'$ with $\sigma _{i+1}\circ \cdots \circ \sigma _ t$ (corollary 9.7.3 ). Hence it has positive order along $E'$. The Jacobians of the other blowups composed with the corresponding maps $ho _ j$ are holomorphic, so they have nonnegative order, and by the chain rule $J_\sigma \circ f'=\prod _ j(J_{\sigma _ j}\circ ho _ j)$. Thus \[  \operatorname {ord}_{E'}(J_\sigma \circ f')\geq 1.  \] Applying ( 9.8.7 ) to $f\circ h=\sigma \circ f'$ and using Step 2, we obtain \[  0\leq \operatorname {ord}_{E'}J_{f'} =k-\operatorname {ord}_{E'}(J_\sigma \circ f')\leq k-1.  \] Step 4: conclusion. The pair $(M',E')$ represents the given prime divisor, as we see on the common smooth model $M'$, and $X'$ is a compact manifold. We repeat Step 3 with $X'$, $f'$ and $E'$ in place of $X$, $f$ and $E$. Each repetition in which the center has codimension at least two lowers the nonnegative integer $k$ by at least one, so after at most $k$ repetitions the center is a hypersurface on the current model, which is obtained from $X$ by a finite sequence of blowups with smooth centers, and it represents the given prime divisor. Finally, by lemma 9.8.5 (2), principalizing the ideal of this hypersurface by theorem 9.6.7 gives a further sequence of blowups with smooth centers, finite because the model is compact, on which the strict transform of the hypersurface is smooth and represents the same prime divisor. $\square$

Let $\Omega \subseteq \mathbb {C}^ n$ ($n\in \mathbb {N}$) be a domain. Definition 5.47.14 . 09A00D4B8096 The plurifine topology on $\Omega $ is the weakest topology making all $\mathbb {R}$-valued plurisubharmonic functions on $\Omega $ continuous. We want to distinguish the Euclidean topology from the plurifine topology. here, topological notions without adjectives refer to those with respect to the Euclidean topology. We include the symbol $\mathcal{F}$ in order to denote those with respect to the plurifine topology. For example, we will say $\mathcal{F}$-open subset, $\mathcal{F}$-neighborhood, $\mathcal{F}$-closure, etc. The $\mathcal{F}$-closure of a set $E\subseteq \Omega $ will be denoted by $\overline{E}^{\mathcal{F}}$. A neighborhood need not be open. Similarly, an $\mathcal F$-neighborhood need not be $\mathcal F$-open. A priori, we should include $\Omega $ into the notations as well, but as we will see shortly in corollary 5.15.14 , this is usually unnecessary. When $n=0$, the domain $\Omega $ is a singleton and all assertions in this subsection are trivial. In the proofs below we therefore assume $n>0$. Proposition 5.15.2 . 0E4FD8AF1256 The plurifine topology on $\Omega $ is finer than the Euclidean topology. Proof. It suffices to show that the unit ball $\{ z\in \mathbb {C}^ n:|z|<1\} $ is $\mathcal{F}$-open. This follows from the observation that this set can be written as \[  \{ \psi <0\}  \quad 	extup{with}\quad \psi (z)\coloneqq (\log |z|) \lor (-1).  \] This completes the proof. $\square$ Example 5.15.3 . 28F2383E9245 Let $\varphi \in \mathrm{PSH}(\Omega )$ and $C\in \mathbb {R}$. Then the sets $\{ \varphi >C\} $ and $\{ \varphi <C\} $ are both $\mathcal{F}$-open. In fact, the latter case follows from proposition 5.15.2 , while the former follows from the observation \[  \{ \varphi >C\} =\{ \varphi \lor (C-1)>C\} .  \] Definition 5.15.4 . EC0662753F9C A subset $E\subseteq \Omega $ is thin 1 at $x\in \Omega $ if one of the following conditions holds: 2F25DFCC541C $x
ot\in \overline{E}$; 9A680A93372A $x\in \overline{E}$ and there is an open neighborhood $U\subseteq \Omega $ of $x$ and $\varphi \in \mathrm{PSH}(U)$ such that \[  \varlimsup _{(U\cap E)\setminus \{ x\}  
i y	o x} \varphi (y)<\varphi (x).  \] We say $E$ is thin if it is thin at all $x\in \Omega $. 1. ↩ Here “thin” means plurithin . Classical thinness, defined using subharmonic functions, is treated separately. Remark 5.15.5 . 3833CFAB6397 In the second case, we can always arrange that \[  \varphi |_{(E\setminus \{ x\} )\cap U}=0.  \] In fact, we may assume that $\varphi \leq 0$ and $C<0$ is such that \[  \varlimsup _{(U\cap E)\setminus \{ x\}  
i y	o x} \varphi (y)<C<\varphi (x).  \] We let \[  \psi =(-C)^{-1}(\varphi \lor C)+1.  \] Then $\psi $ satisfies our requirements for a smaller $U$. In the second case, the function $\varphi $ can be very much improved. Proposition 5.15.6 (Bedford–Taylor) . 80A580DBD009 Consider a set $E\subseteq \Omega $ and $x\in \overline{E}$. Assume that $E$ is thin at $x$. Then there is $\varphi \in \mathrm{PSH}(\mathbb {C}^ n)$ with the following properties: 855F0342BA11 $\varphi $ is locally bounded outside $x$; B158426A08F5 $\varphi (x)>-\infty $; 268FE0CF955A \[  \lim _{E\setminus \{ x\}  
i y	o x} \varphi (y)=-\infty .  \] Proof. By 5.15.5 , there is an open neighborhood $U\subseteq \Omega $ of $x$ and $\psi \in \mathrm{PSH}(U)$ such that \[  \psi |_{U\cap (E\setminus \{ x\} )}=-1 \quad 	extup{and}\quad \psi (x)=0.  \] Without loss of generality we may assume $x=0$ and that $U=B_ n(0,1)$ is the open unit ball. Since $\psi $ is upper semicontinuous at $0$ with $\psi (0)=0$, we may choose a strictly decreasing sequence $(\delta _ j)_{j\geq 1}\subseteq (0,\mathrm{e}^{-1})$ such that 921249630360 5.15.7  egin{equation}  \psi (y)<2^{-j-2}\quad 	extup{whenever } |y|<\delta _ j. \end{equation} In particular $|\log \delta _ j|>1$ for every $j\geq 1$, so the series of constants 1BCCC754A81F 5.15.8  egin{equation}  \sum _{j=1}^\infty  rac{2^{-j-1}}{|\log \delta _ j|} \leq \sum _{j=1}^\infty 2^{-j-1}= rac{1}{2} \end{equation} converges. We further set \[  \gamma _ j\coloneqq \exp  igl (2^{j+1}\log \delta _ j igr )=\delta _ j^{2^{j+1}}\in (0,1),  \] which is also decreasing in $j$. For each $j\geq 1$ define \[  \varphi _ j(z)\coloneqq  egin{cases}   rac{2^{-j-1}}{|\log \delta _ j|}\log |z|\lor  igl (\psi (z)-2^{-j} igr ), & 	extup{if } |z|<\delta _ j,\   rac{2^{-j-1}}{|\log \delta _ j|}\log |z|,& 	extup{if } |z|\geq \delta _ j. \end{cases}  \] On the boundary $|z|=\delta _ j$, the logarithmic term tends to $-2^{-j-1}$. By ( 5.15.7 ), \[  \varlimsup _{|z|<\delta _ j,\  z	o z_0}(\psi (z)-2^{-j})\leq 2^{-j-2}-2^{-j}<-2^{-j-1}  \] for every $z_0$ with $|z_0|=\delta _ j$. Hence the boundary condition in 5.14.16 is satisfied. We obtain that $\varphi _ j\in \mathrm{PSH}(\mathbb {C}^ n)$. By construction, $\varphi _ j\leq 0$ on $U$, $\varphi _ j(0)=-2^{-j}$, and for $z\in E\setminus \{ 0\} $ with $|z|<\gamma _ j$ we have $\varphi _ j(z)\leq -1$, since \[   rac{2^{-j-1}}{|\log \delta _ j|}\log |z|< rac{2^{-j-1}}{|\log \delta _ j|}\, (2^{j+1}\log \delta _ j)=-1  \] and, on $E\setminus \{ 0\} $, $\psi (z)-2^{-j}=-1-2^{-j}<-1$. We then define \[  \varphi \coloneqq \sum _{j=1}^{\infty }\varphi _ j.  \] On the unit ball $U$ each $\varphi _ j\leq 0$, so the partial sums $S_ N\coloneqq \sum _{j=1}^ N\varphi _ j$ form a decreasing sequence of plurisubharmonic functions with \[  S_ N(0)=-\sum _{j=1}^ N 2^{-j}	o -1>-\infty ,\quad 	extup{as } N	o \infty  \] so $\varphi |_ U\in \mathrm{PSH}(U)$ by proposition 5.14.2 . On $\mathbb {C}^ n\setminus B_ n(0,\delta _1)$ each $\varphi _ j$ equals $ rac{2^{-j-1}}{|\log \delta _ j|}\log |z|$, and ( 5.15.8 ) shows $S_ N$ converges uniformly on every compact subset to $ igl (\sum _{j\geq 1}	frac {2^{-j-1}}{|\log \delta _ j|} igr )\log |z|$, a plurisubharmonic limit. Both formulas agree on $U\setminus B_ n(0,\delta _1)$, so altogether $\varphi \in \mathrm{PSH}(\mathbb {C}^ n)$. We now verify the three claims. Property 2 . This is the equality $\varphi (0)=-\sum _{j\geq 1}2^{-j}=-1>-\infty $ obtained above. Property 3 . Fix $j_0\geq 1$. For $z\in E\setminus \{ 0\} $ with $|z|<\gamma _{j_0}$, the bound $\varphi _ j(z)\leq -1$ holds for every $j\leq j_0$ (since the $\gamma _ j$ are decreasing), while $\varphi _ j(z)\leq 0$ for $j>j_0$. Hence $\varphi (z)\leq -j_0$, and letting $j_0	o \infty $ yields \[  \varlimsup _{E\setminus \{ 0\}  
