eq 0$ was arbitrary, $\varphi $ is locally bounded on $\mathbb {C}^ n\setminus \{ 0\} $, which is property 1 . $\square$ Lemma 5.15.9 . D3F2BF3D82BD Let $E_1,E_2\subseteq \Omega $. Assume that $E_1$, $E_2$ are both thin at $x\in \Omega $. Then so is $E_1\cup E_2$. Proof. We may assume that $x\in \overline{E_1}\cap \overline{E_2}$. Take an open neighborhood $U\subseteq \Omega $ of $x$ and $\varphi _1,\varphi _2\in \mathrm{PSH}(U)$ such that \[  \varlimsup _{y	o x,y\in E_ i\setminus \{ x\} } \varphi _ i(y)<\varphi _ i(x),\quad i=1,2.  \] Then $\varphi _1+\varphi _2\in \mathrm{PSH}(U)$ and \[  \varlimsup _{y	o x,y\in (E_1\cup E_2)\setminus \{ x\} } (\varphi _1+\varphi _2)(y)<\varphi _1(x)+\varphi _2(x).  \] In particular, $E_1\cup E_2$ is thin at $x$. $\square$ Theorem 5.15.10 (H. Cartan) . 458C93F5F6CD Consider a set $E\subseteq \Omega $ and $x\in E$. Then the following are equivalent: 15B95CB674C7 $E$ is an $\mathcal{F}$-neighborhood of $x$; 53048930270F $\Omega \setminus E$ is thin at $x$. Proof. 2 $\implies $ 1 . Assume 2 . We may assume that $x\in \overline{\Omega \setminus E}$. Otherwise, our assertion follows from proposition 5.15.2 . By proposition 5.15.6 , there is an open neighborhood $U$ of $x$ in $\Omega $ and $\varphi \in \mathrm{PSH}(\mathbb {C}^ n)$ such that \[  \varphi (x)>\sup _{y\in U\cap (\Omega \setminus E)} \varphi (y)\eqqcolon \lambda .  \] Let $F=\{ y\in \Omega : \varphi (y)>\lambda \} $. Then $x\in F$ and $F$ is $\mathcal{F}$-open by example 5.15.3 . Moreover, $U\cap F\subseteq E$. By proposition 5.15.2 , we conclude 1 . 1 $\implies $ 2 . Assume 1 . We may always replace $E$ by smaller $\mathcal{F}$-neighborhoods of $x$. In particular, we may assume that $E$ has the following form \[  \{ y\in U:\varphi _1(y)>\lambda _1,\ldots ,\varphi _ m(y)>\lambda _ m\} ,  \] where $U\subseteq \Omega $ is an open neighborhood of $x$, and $\varphi _1,\ldots ,\varphi _ m$ are $\mathbb {R}$-valued psh functions on $\Omega $, and $\lambda _1,\ldots ,\lambda _ m\in \mathbb {R}$. Since $x\in U$, the set $\Omega \setminus U$ is thin at $x$. Moreover, \[  \Omega \setminus E=(\Omega \setminus U)\cup  igcup _{i=1}^ m\{ y\in U:\varphi _ i(y)\leq \lambda _ i\} .  \] By 5.15.9 , it is enough to prove that each set $\{ y\in U:\varphi _ i(y)\leq \lambda _ i\} $ is thin at $x$. Thus we may assume $m=1$. In this case, $\Omega \setminus E$ is clearly thin at $x$. $\square$ Theorem 5.15.11 . 4388B85DDD67 A base of the plurifine topology on $\Omega $ is given by sets of the following form: E3CEA68870B6 5.15.12  egin{equation}  \left\{ x\in U: \varphi (x)>0ight\} , \end{equation} where $U\subseteq \Omega $ is an open subset and $\varphi \in \mathrm{PSH}(U)$. Proof. Observe that sets of the form ( 5.15.12 ) are $\mathcal{F}$-open. 2 By theorem 5.15.10 , it suffices to show its complement in $\Omega $ is thin at each point of ( 5.15.12 ), which is obvious. Now consider $x\in \Omega $ and an $\mathcal{F}$-open neighborhood $V\subseteq \Omega $ of $x$. We want to find a set of the form ( 5.15.12 ) contained in $V$ and containing $x$. Write $E=\Omega \setminus V$. In case $x\in \operatorname{Int}V$, there is nothing to prove. So we may assume that $x\in \overline{E}$. By theorem 5.15.10 , $E$ is thin at $x$. By definition, there is an open neighborhood $U\subseteq \Omega $ of $x$ and $\varphi \in \mathrm{PSH}(U)$ such that \[  \varlimsup _{y	o x,y\in U\cap (E\setminus \{ x\} )}\varphi (y)<\varphi (x).  \] We may assume that $\varphi |_{E\cap U}\leq 0<\varphi (x)$. Then the set $\{ y\in U:\varphi (y)>0\} $ suffices for our purpose. $\square$ 2. ↩ This is not entirely obvious by definition, as $\varphi $ is not defined on the whole $\Omega $. Remark 5.15.13 . 2EDAD1EF1694 Recall that in general, an $\mathcal{F}$-open set is not a countable union of sets of the form ( 5.15.12 ). In fact, an $\mathcal{F}$-open set is not a Borel set in general. See [ EK23 ] for a concrete example. Corollary 5.15.14 . 49909C9A34E2 Let $\Omega _1\subseteq \Omega _2\s