edge \cdots \wedge \theta _{j,\psi _ j}. \end{equation} Proof. Observe that $\varphi \lor \psi \in \mathcal{E}(X,\theta ;\phi )$, as a consequence of the monotonicity theorem theorem 13.13.11 . Using the plurifine locality of the non-pluripolar product proposition 13.11.4 1 , we compute \[  \begin{aligned} & \int _ X \theta _{\varphi \lor \psi }^{n-j}\wedge \theta _{1,\psi _1}\wedge \cdots \wedge \theta _{j,\psi _ j}\\  \geq &  \int _{\{ \varphi >\psi \}  } \theta _{\varphi }^{n-j}\wedge \theta _{1,\psi _1}\wedge \cdots \wedge \theta _{j,\psi _ j}+\int _{\{ \varphi <\psi \}  } \theta _{\psi }^{n-j}\wedge \theta _{1,\psi _1}\wedge \cdots \wedge \theta _{j,\psi _ j}\\  =&  \int _ X \theta _{\varphi }^{n-j}\wedge \theta _{1,\psi _1}\wedge \cdots \wedge \theta _{j,\psi _ j}-\int _{\{ \varphi \leq \psi \}  } \theta _{\varphi }^{n-j}\wedge \theta _{1,\psi _1}\wedge \cdots \wedge \theta _{j,\psi _ j}\\ & +\int _{\{ \varphi <\psi \}  } \theta _{\psi }^{n-j}\wedge \theta _{1,\psi _1}\wedge \cdots \wedge \theta _{j,\psi _ j}\\  =& \int _ X \theta _{\varphi \lor \psi }^{n-j}\wedge \theta _{1,\psi _1}\wedge \cdots \wedge \theta _{j,\psi _ j}-\int _{\{ \varphi \leq \psi \}  } \theta _{\varphi }^{n-j}\wedge \theta _{1,\psi _1}\wedge \cdots \wedge \theta _{j,\psi _ j}\\ & +\int _{\{ \varphi <\psi \}  } \theta _{\psi }^{n-j}\wedge \theta _{1,\psi _1}\wedge \cdots \wedge \theta _{j,\psi _ j}, \end{aligned}  \] where in the last step, we applied proposition 16.3.10 to $\varphi $ and $\varphi \lor \psi $. Therefore, \[  \begin{aligned}  \int _{\{ \varphi \leq \psi \} }&  \theta _{\varphi }^{n-j} \wedge \theta _{1,\psi _1}\wedge \cdots \wedge \theta _{j,\psi _ j}\\ & \geq \int _{\{ \varphi <\psi \} } \theta _{\psi }^{n-j} \wedge \theta _{1,\psi _1}\wedge \cdots \wedge \theta _{j,\psi _ j}. \end{aligned}  \] Applying the preceding inequality to $\varphi +\epsilon $ in place of $\varphi $, and using $\theta _{\varphi +\epsilon }=\theta _\varphi $, we get \[  \int _{\{ \varphi +\epsilon \leq \psi \} }\theta _\varphi ^{n-j}\wedge \theta _{1,\psi _1}\wedge \cdots \wedge \theta _{j,\psi _ j} \geq \int _{\{ \varphi +\epsilon <\psi \} }\theta _\psi ^{n-j}\wedge \theta _{1,\psi _1}\wedge \cdots \wedge \theta _{j,\psi _ j}.  \] As $\epsilon \searrow 0$, by monotone convergence theorem, we obtain \[  \int _{\{ \varphi <\psi \} }\theta _\varphi ^{n-j}\wedge \theta _{1,\psi _1}\wedge \cdots \wedge \theta _{j,\psi _ j} \geq \int _{\{ \varphi <\psi \} }\theta _\psi ^{n-j}\wedge \theta _{1,\psi _1}\wedge \cdots \wedge \theta _{j,\psi _ j},  \] which proves ( 16.5.6 ). Finally, the ( 16.5.7 ) follows by subtracting ( 16.5.6 ) (with the role of $\varphi $ and $\psi $ switched) from the total mixed mass equality \[  \int _ X\theta _\varphi ^{n-j}\wedge \theta _{1,\psi _1}\wedge \cdots \wedge \theta _{j,\psi _ j}= \int _ X\theta _\psi ^{n-j}\wedge \theta _{1,\psi _1}\wedge \cdots \wedge \theta _{j,\psi _ j},  \] which itself follows from proposition 16.3.10 and proposition 16.5.4 . $\square$ The full mass potentials are essential in resolving the Monge–Ampère equations. We recall the following two Aubin–Yau type theorems. Recall that a measure $\mu $ on $X$ is non-pluripolar if it is a Radon measure and $\mu (K)=0$ for each pluripolar set $K\subseteq X$. 1 Theorem 16.5.8 . 6F1A42885C28 Let $\mu $ be a non-pluripolar measure on $X$ with $\mu (X)=\int _ X \theta _{\phi }^ n$. Then there is a unique $\varphi \in \mathcal{E}(X,\theta ;\phi )$ such that \[  \theta _{\varphi }^ n=\mu ,\quad \sup _ X \varphi =0.  \] 1. ↩ Here we identify as usual a Radon measure with its completion. Theorem 16.5.9 . 0E437FB98B97 Fix $\lambda >0$. Let $\mu $ be a non-pluripolar measure on $X$ with $\mu (X)>0$. Then there is a unique $\varphi \in \mathcal{E}(X,\theta ;\phi )$ such that \[  \theta _{\varphi }^ n=\mathrm{e}^{\lambda \varphi }\mu .  \] Furthermore, for any $\psi \in \mathcal{E}(X,\theta ;\phi )$ satisfying \[  \theta _{\psi }^ n\geq \mathrm{e}^{\lambda \psi }\mu ,  \] we have $\varphi \geq \psi $. The proofs are beyond the scope of this book; we refer to [ DDNL19log ] . 


